add(
  center("ABSTRACT", { size: 30, bold: true, before: 200, after: 300 }),
  P("A voice agent answers a caller by converting speech to text, retrieving relevant passages with that transcript, and composing an answer from them. Retrieval therefore runs on what the agent heard, not on what the caller said, while the neural rerankers inside these pipelines are trained on typed text. This project studies how much spoken queries damage retrieval, whether deep-learning methods can make the reranker more robust, and what this means for a real voice agent."),
  P("We first built a production-grade re-ranking stage into an existing voice support agent: a cross-encoder (MiniLM-L6, 22.7M parameters) re-orders the top-25 BM25 candidates, with a safe fallback to BM25 on any failure. We then evaluated this pipeline, alongside dense and hybrid retrievers, in two settings. Part 1 uses spoken versions of three BEIR collections from science, nutrition/medicine and finance (1,271 queries), synthesised in five English accents and transcribed by Whisper over wideband audio and a simulated telephone line. Part 2 uses real human speech from SD-QA: 747 Wikipedia questions read by speakers of eleven English dialects, including North and South Indian English, which we converted into a passage-retrieval benchmark of 46,185 passages and 21 test conditions."),
  P("Spoken queries reduced nDCG@10 by 7–25% over wideband audio, 24–58% over the telephone, 11.2% on real speech and 16.9% for real Indian-English speech, and the reranker did not reduce the relative loss. In a controlled fine-tuning study with three seeds and two checkpoint-selection protocols, fine-tuning the reranker on clean in-domain text made it more fragile to speech; training on ASR transcripts with relevance labels repaired part of the damage but never beat the original model. Label-free consistency distillation, where the reranker learns to score a transcript the way the original model scores the clean question, avoided the damage: it was never significantly worse than the original, was significantly better than clean fine-tuning on two of three collections, and was the best reranker on every real-speech group, although its gain over the original was small and not significant after correction. The largest gain on real speech came from the first stage: re-ranking a hybrid BM25 + dense candidate list instead of a BM25 list raised nDCG@10 from 0.516 to 0.607. For Indian-English speakers, the localised en-IN recogniser gave significantly worse retrieval than the US model. A pre-registered follow-up confirmed the fine-tuning findings on a modern 278M-parameter reranker (bge-reranker-base), where consistency distillation significantly beat the original model in 7 of 9 settings, An error analysis showed that misrecognised content words — not function words — drive the loss. Pre-registered tests on three new real-speech test sets confirmed that a hybrid first stage helps and that distillation from a larger teacher significantly improves even the small reranker; training on real rather than synthetic speech errors did not help. Re-ranking 25 passages takes about 1.1 s on CPU and 80 ms on a T4 GPU."),
  P([{ t: "Keywords: ", bold: true }, { t: "voice agents, spoken queries, speech recognition, passage retrieval, cross-encoder reranking, fine-tuning, knowledge distillation, robustness, English dialects", italics: true }]),
  pageBreak(),
);

